From 8b2c20e9047e53aa62e1b5221af58a4207804472 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Spanier <spanier.michael@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:22:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 31/43] net80211: roam sooner from a weak or lost AP

A walk through the house with the first roaming driver showed the
station clinging to a fading AP: 25 s at a signal of 5 to 10 on the
stairs, then the link dropped and DHCP started over. In other places
the switch came only once the old AP was all but gone.

Several things kept it there:

- Missed beacons make the iwm send a probe request, which arms
  ic_mgt_timer. ieee80211_begin_bgscan() refuses to scan while that
  timer runs; without an answer the watchdog drops to SCAN after 5 s.
  While the timer is pending in RUN, scan anyway: the scan answers
  the same question and may find a better AP.
- A refused scan request still counted as a scan, so the next try
  waited a full gap. Retry after 500 ms instead.
- A weak AP was easily missed by the scan itself. end_scan() then
  went to a full scan. While frames from it still come in, decide by
  its smoothed signal and roam to the best AP of the scan.
- Below 30 % the dwell time and the backoff kept scans away for up
  to a minute. Scan every 2.5 s (every second below 20 %), or up to
  every 10 s while the signal stays put.
- A weak AP is left after one scan for an AP 15 better. A lost AP
  is left at once for one that is not lost and 4 better, not for one
  as weak, so that all-weak rooms do not cause hopping.

The tests replay the four bad stretches of the walk: the switches
come after 33, 24, 17 and 4 s instead of 50, a lost link, 23 and
39 s. Standing still still gives no switches.

Signed-off-by: Michael Spanier <spanier.michael@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: claude-opus-5-5
